# Agent coordination

- Before writing, identify the exact Git root, file scope, active agents and
  uncommitted overlap. One repository has at most one active writer.
- Prefer direct messaging between existing Codex tasks. Name the repository,
  branch/worktree, current state, files or area, requested action, and the next
  potentially conflicting action.
- `/kom <agent-or-task> <message>` is human-facing shorthand for that direct
  task contact. Use the project skill when it is loaded; otherwise use the
  available task channel, such as `send_message_to_thread`. It is not a shell
  command, product protocol, or reason to create a duplicate agent.
- The receiver acknowledges scope or a write lock and reports commit/checks or
  a blocker. Follow progress through task status/waiting rather than starting
  the same work elsewhere.
- Messages do not authorize secrets access, cross-repository mutation, push,
  merge, deployment, or external-resource changes. Ask Jakob if no intended
  recipient can be identified safely.
- If direct messaging is unavailable, write a secret-free temporary note under
  `.agents/coordination/<agent-id>.md` and tell the user that delivery is not
  live. Remove or mark it complete when ownership ends.
- On overlap, pause the overlapping edit and agree on ownership, ordering or a
  compatible split. Never overwrite, stage, commit or rewrite another agent's
  work without agreement.
- Use repository authority, typed contracts and tests when one option is
  clearly stronger. Ask Jakob when materially different options remain about
  equally defensible or change security, data, product scope or cloud state.
